**Direct answer to the user's question on "legacy_migrated" / the migration scaffolding (from the "6 -> 5" paste, "No half measures, remember?"):** The `migrate_panel_state_from_kvp` / `has_panel_migration_marker` / `legacy_migrated` / one-time termination hook + delete of the old KVP entry is **something we (the surmount branch team) invented along the way** for ROADMAP-01 (full-commitment termination of the old per-workspace KVP path for ZT-1 panel state so the new heed3 + rkyv kv backend in ThreadMetadataStore with the agent_panels table becomes the sole path). It is **not** present in original upstream Zed. It exists because we chose "full commitment, no hybrid" to the new kv backend (the old KVP path for this data is to be terminated, not carried forward, per the binding Long-term Branch Reality and "zero interest in legacy unperformant code" directives). The hook does a one-time backfill from the old KVP (under AGENT_PANEL_KEY) into the new kv path on first load after cutover, writes a marker for idempotency, and deletes the old entry. The `legacy_migrated` flag (computed in the synchronous pre-spawn block where real cx is available) is used to condition the delete inside the background_spawn (where the .await is legal). Per the user's prior Option B selection (hard eventual removal after defined cutoff) and the explicit "No half measures, remember?" + "Why are we migrating at all? Not sure it makes sense to have that or keep that" in this paste, this scaffolding should not be kept around as "temporary." The next autonomous steps (per "keep going then") will include more aggressive cleanup/excision of the termination hook itself (or setting the hard cutoff date now) rather than polishing the legacy path further. All decisions are documented clearly for future heavy GPL Zed merges (Long-term Branch Reality).

